WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.290
Build a budget that works, and bends in hard months.

2
00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:08.510
This is the video for Module 3, Budgeting: Planning Your Money Month by Month.

3
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:17.480
A budget is a plan for your money, made before you spend it. This much is coming in, and this is exactly where it will go.

4
00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:26.620
Tracking looks back at what happened. A budget looks forward. Together they form a loop: plan, spend, track, compare, improve.

5
00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:37.620
A budget is not a punishment or a sign that you are poor. It means you decide in advance, calmly, instead of at the market or when a relative calls.

6
00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:45.290
A popular guideline is 50 percent for needs, 30 percent for wants, and 20 percent for savings.

7
00:00:45.640 --> 00:01:00.110
For example, Zainab, a nurse in Abuja, takes home about 250,000 naira a month. The classic split gives needs 125,000, wants 75,000 and savings 50,000.

8
00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:10.660
But her real needs, including her mother's medicine, are about 175,000. That is 70 percent, and that is not failure.

9
00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:21.400
So she cuts wants to 25,000 and keeps savings at 50,000. She protects her savings by trimming wants, not by pretending her needs are smaller.

10
00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:31.100
Write your expected income at the top. Use take-home pay, or your safe figure if income is irregular. Then write savings first.

11
00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:39.600
Then fixed needs. Turn yearly costs like rent into a monthly line, so the money is waiting when the rent date comes.

12
00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:44.050
Then variable needs, using your tracking as a guide.

13
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:56.480
Then a realistic giving line for church or mosque, family and events. Wants should be modest but not zero. And add a buffer of about 5 percent for things you forgot.

14
00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:06.410
If the total is more than income, cut wants first, then trim variable needs. If it still does not balance, income may need to rise.

15
00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:12.860
Do not borrow for everyday needs. That only moves the problem to next month, with interest added.

16
00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:20.260
For example, Tolu and her husband in Lagos take home about 400,000 naira a month.

17
00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:27.670
Savings go first: 30,000 for the emergency fund and 30,000 for school fees.

18
00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:40.530
Their annual rent is about 1,200,000 naira. Divided by 12, that is 100,000 a month into a rent pot, plus 10,000 for the cooperative.

19
00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:54.220
Food, transport, electricity, gas, data and toiletries come to 186,000. Food is the biggest line, so a weekly menu and bulk staples make a big difference.

20
00:02:55.470 --> 00:03:00.480
Giving is 20,000, the buffer 10,000, and wants get 14,000.

21
00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:10.350
It adds up to exactly 400,000 naira. Savings and rent are protected first, and wants get what is realistically left.

22
00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:17.460
On payday, move savings, rent savings and school fees savings into their own pots first.

23
00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:31.090
Divide food and transport into weekly amounts. If food is 80,000 naira a month, give yourself about 20,000 each week. Overspending shows up in week one, while there is still time.

24
00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:48.210
Then spend 10 minutes each week writing actual spending next to the plan. For example, Ese in Benin City was 6,000 naira over on food. She moved 3,000 from transport and 3,000 from wants, and her savings stayed untouched.

25
00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:58.180
A good budget bends without breaking. Protect food, shelter and health first, then the costs that keep income coming. Cut wants first.

26
00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:11.890
For example, Ngozi sells clothes in Onitsha. She usually plans on about 150,000 naira, but expects only 110,000 this month. She cuts wants, saving 12,000.

27
00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:19.260
She reduces giving and explains to family that this is a tight month. That saves 7,000.

28
00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:25.180
She cooks in bulk and buys staples with a neighbour, saving 6,000 on food.

29
00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:36.250
She reduces her goal savings by 15,000, but keeps paying rent savings in full. Together that closes the 40,000 gap without borrowing.

30
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:47.500
Turn on transaction alerts. Never share your PIN, password, OTP or BVN with anyone, including someone who says they are from your bank.

31
00:04:47.850 --> 00:05:00.160
Scams use urgent messages, sometimes from hacked accounts of friends. It is fine to say you will check your budget and reply in the evening. And avoid quick loans for everyday food and bills.

32
00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:10.220
Now choose your method, write your first budget, run your first weekly check, and prepare a hard-month budget. Continue with the lessons below.
